00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
hey guys welcome to max tech i've been  using and testing the iphone 13 pro max  for just about two weeks now and  although this is by far the most happy  i've ever been with an iphone due to it  just being so polished there are still  some software and hardware problems that  i'm going to be discussing in this video  as well as the solutions to some of  those problems so let's get right into  it the first problem has to do with  overheating issues which can cause the  display to dim down which are the same  issues we had before with the iphone 12  pro max for example max the other main  host of this channel was doing some real  estate drone videography yesterday with  his iphone 13 pro as the display  for his mavic air 2 drone and he found  that after only a few minutes out in the  sun the display dimmed down to 50  percent of the maximum brightness making  it difficult to see the display and it  wouldn't even recover now this issue is  likely due to being out in direct  sunlight while requiring high brightness  to see the display and actively using  gps to track and control the drone now i  do want to mention that the dimming  issues aren't nearly as bad as last  year's 12 pro max which dimmed down to  an extremely low level probably twice as  dim as the 13 pro max but it still sucks  to see that we have to deal with dimming  like this now i also want to mention  that this doesn't just apply to drone  videography because tyler stallman a  photographer who runs a youtube channel  also noticed that the 13 still dims  while taking photos out in the sun but  thankfully not as bad as a 12. but with  that said we actually did find a fix for  the display dimming problem and that is  low power mode which clocks down the  speed of the a15 bionic chip so it uses  less power and produces less heat so max  tested his drone out one more time  except now with low power mode turned on  and the brightness manually turned down  to 80 percent and after 15 to 20 minutes  of using his drone outside he didn't  experience any dimming at all and was  able to use his drone without any issues  even without having to go into the shade  and to turn this mode on just go into  your settings app go to the control  center then tap the plus icon next to  low power mode then bring down your  control panel by pulling down from the  battery icon and then simply tap on the  low power mode icon to turn it on now  getting into the second problem with the  13 pro max this one has to do with the  software ever since i got this phone  i've noticed an issue where sometimes my  touch inputs don't seem to register  causing me to have to tab multiple times  to get into a link and for some reason  i'm mostly experiencing this issue  within the youtube app when i try to tap  on videos and they simply won't load  until i move the display around and come  back to the video now i do want to  mention that i experienced this issue  with my 12 pro max while running ios 15  beta software so i'm convinced that it's  an issue to do with ios 15 but even  after updating to the latest ios  15.0.1 update i'm still getting this  touch input reliability issue so here's  a quick tip on how to deal with this  until apple gives it a proper update  just scroll down slightly and tap in a  different location which works for me  sometimes now getting into problem  number three this actually has to do  with the cameras on the 13 pro and the  pro max the new macro mode is definitely  an awesome addition because it allows  you to take photos that were never  possible before on an iphone allowing  you to get much closer than you can on  iphones without this new feature but  there's actually a pretty bad problem  with the macro mode specifically when  you start getting closer to an object  the system automatically switches to the  macro lens for you without letting you  manually choose the lens yourself but  the even bigger issue is that it  switches to that macro lens way too soon  because it tries to keep the transition  looking smooth so it actually uses a  digital crop on the ultra wide lens so  it matches the composition but then if  you tap the 0.5 x button it reveals just  how much digital crop it was adding to  make it look like a smooth transition  but that digital crop is ruining the  image quality for those who didn't  really want to get super close to the  subject and just snap the photo at a  medium distance so if apple allowed you  to switch the cameras manually perhaps  to the telephoto lens the photo would  end up turning out much nicer at that  medium distance but thankfully there is  a way to solve this issue until apple  releases an update you actually have to  physically cover the ultrawide lens with  your finger allowing you to choose the  other lenses to take a shot like the  telephoto which gives you more  background compression getting into  problem number four the camera system on  the 13 pro sometimes chooses to use the  telephoto lens for low light shots when  it really shouldn't be during our  unbiased camera comparison between the  13 pro max and the s21 ultra we took a  photo in very low light and the iphone  did much worse than the samsung that's  because the iphone chose to use its 3x  telephoto lens to take the shot while  the s21 ultra actually cropped in three  times on the main camera which obviously  looks much better now the reason for  that is because the main lens has a much  faster aperture which lets in much more  light so cropping in three times on the  main lens actually looks better and the  worst part is that on the latest iphone  13 pro and pro max the 3x telephoto lens  now has an even slower f 2.8 aperture  than the telephoto lenses on the 11 and  12 pro max which can actually cause  nighttime photos to look less detailed  because it's taking in less light so it  seems as though the software thinks that  using the 3x lens and those dark  scenarios will give you more detail but  it doesn't so users who don't know  what's going on are gonna end up with  noticeably worse photos compared to  someone who does understand all of this  and will know that they've gotta switch  to the main lens to get the best quality  especially since the main lens supports  night mode portraits combining both of  those features and speaking of night  mode the 13 pro is the first iphone  which supports night mode directly on  the telephoto lens but the major issue  is that because of the slower f 2.8  aperture the photos can actually turn  out worse than the 11 and 12 pro max  which use a crop of the main lens and  that is because the slower aperture  means that you have to wait longer for  the sensor to gather light and the  longer you have to wait the more camera  shake gets added to the image as well as  the movements in the subject itself like  these swaying trees that end up looking  a bit blurry so if you're going to be  shooting night mode photos with the  telephoto lens i actually recommend  zooming out a slight bit from 3x to 2.9x  zoom forcing it to use the main lens  instead which actually ends up looking  better than the telephoto lens now the  fifth problem has to do with gaming  especially for games that are more  graphically intensive i've been testing  out multiple games like call of duty  mobile pokemon unite league of legends  wild rift pubg and genshin impact and  most of these games run fine without any  display dimming and overheating issues  but i started to notice some dimming in  pubg and then a bunch of dimming and  gentian impact since it's the most  graphically demanding ios game out there  specifically it would dim down to just  over 50 brightness after only 5 to 10  minutes of gameplay at maxed out  settings now these results are  definitely improved compared to the 12  pro max which would dim much more and  after a shorter period of time but still  it sucks to see that apple just can't  keep the a15 chip cool enough to avoid  these display dimming issues however we  discovered a fix using low power mode is  actually a viable option for gaming so i  ended up making a full video testing an  hour of gaming both with and without low  power mode being enabled and i  discovered that you'll save a ton of  battery life without sacrificing  performance since it's stuck to 60fps  for the four games that we tested and  then in generation impact low power mode  allowed the iphone to last a full 20  minutes before dimming to the 50  brightness level compared to only 5 to  10 minutes without low power mode  enabled now the only issue with this  mode is that it actually limits the  display refresh rate from 120 hertz down  to a maximum of 60 so you essentially  wouldn't be able to use it if you wanted  to play games at 120fps for example i  played pascal's wager at 120fps and the  phone dimmed like crazy after only a few  minutes because of the massive graphics  load of trying to draw so many frames so  apple definitely needs to add better  cooling to the a16 chip next year to  solve this problem but for 60fps games  low power mode is a must and if you're  experiencing display dimming issues you  can try to start your gameplay session  off at 80 percent or lower brightness to  reduce heat and you can also try turning  down some of the graphics settings in  your game as well problem number six  brings us back to the cameras  specifically the cinematic video  recording mode first off just the fact  that it's limited to 1080p instead of 4k  means that i'm basically not gonna want  to be recording in this mode because i'd  rather have the much better 4k footage  and since the blur edging is still a bit  rough around the edges you kind of have  to turn down the blur amount to make it  look more natural but then the other  problem is that this mode is locked into  30fps recording which honestly is a bit  weird since many cinematographers use  24fps video recording including most  movies in fact almost every single video  we shoot on this channel is shot at 24  fps with the only exception being our  60fps gaming comparison videos which are  shot that way to show differences in  frame rates while gaming so apple really  should have given us a toggle to at  least switch between 24 and 30 fps for  those who want it but unfortunately they  didn't getting into problem number seven  apple did reveal the prores video  recording feature which ups the bit rate  to give cleaner looking video footage  but there are some big problems with  that first off since the file sizes are  so large specifically 6 gigabytes per  minute for 4k recording you need at  least a 256 gigabyte model to be able to  record at 4k but making it worse the  iphone still packs a lightning port  which is extremely slow at transferring  data since it uses the old usb 2.0  standard now michael tobin who got an  exclusive first look at the iphone 13  pro's prores video recording feature  said it took him an hour and a half to  transfer almost 200 gigabytes of prores  clips using the lightning port which is  extremely slow for only around 33  minutes of footage much lower than usb  type c speeds now michael does recommend  using the image capture app on a mac to  transfer the clips because it transfers  one clip at a time so if anything fails  or you need to unplug it it's not going  to ruin the entire transfer like it will  on the photos app now finishing off with  problem number eight the iphone 13 pro  max is missing a very important feature  that android phones have had for years  ever since they switched to oled  displays yes that's the rumored always  on display feature that people thought  apple would be adding ever since the  first oled iphone 10 but no and now with  apple finally giving the iphone 13 pro  an ltpo display capable of refreshing  from 10 hertz all the way up to 120  hertz people thought that we're finally  getting this feature on this iphone  because it's the perfect time to add it  but for some reason apple didn't and to  make things even worse there are no  excuses left because the display is now  25 more efficient than the one on the 12  pro and on top of that the battery life  in these new iphones is insane but apple  simply chose not to add always on for  some reason so my only guess is that  apple intentionally left this feature  out to make the next iphone seem better  or maybe they just didn't think it was  polished enough to add it with this  phone so there you guys go those are the  top eight problems that we've had with  the iphone 13 pro and if you have any  more to add then go ahead and comment  them down below and if any of those tips  helped you out then click the circle  above to subscribe for more videos like  this one and definitely check those two  videos out right over there thanks for  watching and we'll see you in the next  video  [Music]  you

